# Decoding Safety Feedback from Diverse Raters: A Data-driven Lens on Responsiveness to Severity

Pushkar Mishra, Charvi Rastogi, Stephen R. Pfohl, Alicia Parrish, Tian Huey Teh, Roma Patel, Mark Diaz, Ding Wang, Michela Paganini, Vinodkumar Prabhakaran, Lora Aroyo, Verena Rieser

arXiv 2025

## TL;DR

This paper introduces a novel, data-driven, non-parametric framework to evaluate how effectively human raters use ordinal scales (like Likert scales) to report AI safety violations. By defining two new metrics—Monotonic Precision Area (MPA) and Weighted Recall Area (WRA)—the authors expose significant demographic variations in safety perceptions, offering a robust tool to improve pluralistic AI alignment and rater selection.

## What Problem Does This Paper Address?

Aligning generative AI models with human values requires nuanced safety feedback. While binary "safe/unsafe" labels are too blunt to capture the severity of potential harms, fine-grained ordinal scales introduce complex human response biases, such as extreme responding or central tendency. This is captured by the Weighted Recall Area (WRA), which evaluates the concordance probability at each score.

The Harmonic Mean (HM) of MPA and WRA provides a single unified responsiveness score. Crucially, this method is agnostic to the ground-truth reference, meaning raters can be evaluated against strict expert guidelines ($V^g$) or against the collective crowd consensus ($V^c$).

## Datasets

The framework was evaluated using two distinct public datasets:

  * Dataset 1 (Image Safety): A dataset from Rastogi et al. (2024) containing safety ratings on prompt-image pairs. It includes binary evaluations from expert raters and 0–4 Likert scale ratings from a diverse crowd structured by trisectional demographic identities (combinations of ethnicity, age, and gender).
  * Dataset 2 (Text Toxicity): A dataset from Kumar et al. (2021) consisting of 107,620 comments evaluated on a 0–4 toxicity scale by diverse raters categorized by their religious leaning, age, and sexuality.

## Key Insights and Ideas

  * Disentangling Scale Usage from Perception: Raters often use scales differently due to cultural backgrounds or response biases (e.g., a group might only use scores 0 and 4). --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
